// RETRACTION ON THE STATIC INCREMENTAL PATH, CHECKED THREE WAYS.
//
// `deem!` now emits, for an aggregate query whose every aggregate can RUN
// BACKWARDS, a delta primitive that carries a SOURCE TAG and a WEIGHT:
//
//     let mut h: QaIncr = qa_incremental();
//     qa_apply(&mut h, 0i32,  1i64, rows)?;    // a +1 delta over source 0
//     qa_apply(&mut h, 0i32, -1i64, rows)?;    // …and its inverse
//     qa_epoch(&mut h, rows)?;                 // = qa_apply(h, 0, +1, rows)
//     qa_retract(&mut h, rows)?;               // = qa_apply(h, 0, -1, rows)
//     let out = qa_snapshot(&h)?;
//
// ⚠ THREE WITNESSES, BECAUSE "BOTH ENGINES AGREE" IS NOT SUFFICIENT — the same
// discipline `wql_incr_static_three_ways` states and for the same measured
// reason. Every number below is asserted against all three of:
//
//   (i)   HAND-DERIVED CONSTANTS, written out in the table below;
//   (ii)  the static BATCH fn `qa()` over the REMAINING multiset, which is the
//         correctness spec a retraction is judged against (a handle that has
//         seen I then R must answer what a batch over I∖R answers);
//   (iii) the DYNAMIC interpreter's incremental engine driven by a raw signed
//         `ZBatch`, a genuinely separate implementation over `RtVal`.
//
// ⚠ WITNESS (iii) IS THE WASTING ASSET. The interpreter still owns retraction
// and is scheduled for deletion; while it exists the static answer can be
// checked against it, and after that no later slice can be validated this way.
//
// ── THE DATA AND THE ANSWERS, BY HAND ──────────────────────────────────────
//
// A. `qa` — count + i64 sum. Rows in this order (group order is FIRST
//    OCCURRENCE, so g = 0, 1, 2):
//        (0, 5) (1, 10) (0, -3) (2, -8) (0, 6) (2, 2) (0, 8)
//
//      after the INSERT epoch          after RETRACT { (0,-3), (2,2) }
//        g=0  c=4  t = 5-3+6+8 = 16      g=0  c=3  t = 5+6+8 = 19
//        g=1  c=1  t = 10                g=1  c=1  t = 10
//        g=2  c=2  t = -8+2 = -6         g=2  c=1  t = -8
//
//    The remaining multiset is { (0,5) (1,10) (2,-8) (0,6) (0,8) } and its
//    first-occurrence group order is 0, 1, 2 — the same order, which is what
//    makes the comparison against `qa()` assertable ELEMENT BY ELEMENT.
//
//      then RETRACT { (1,10) } — the EMPTIED GROUP
//        g=0  c=3  t = 19
//        g=1  c=0  →  ABSENT from the snapshot
//        g=2  c=1  t = -8
//
//    ⚠ ABSENT, NOT ZERO, AND THAT IS MEASURED OFF THE INTERPRETER RATHER THAN
//    CHOSEN. `logos.mem.deem.incr` keeps the slot (`keyv`/`gcount`/`gsum` all
//    persist) and sets `live = false` when the multiplicity reaches 0: the row
//    leaves the output, first-occurrence order is untouched, and a re-inserted
//    key finds its old slot. The static tier reproduces that with a guard in
//    `<q>_snapshot` — the group is TOMBSTONED, not deleted. The remaining
//    multiset is { (0,5) (2,-8) (0,6) (0,8) }, whose batch order is 0, 2.
//
// B. `qu` — count + `u64` sum, AT THE EXTREME THAT SEPARATES THE PREDICATES.
//        insert (0, 2^63) and (0, 5)  →  n=2  t = 9223372036854775813
//        retract (0, 5)               →  n=1  t = 9223372036854775808 = 2^63
//
//    2^63 EXACTLY. This is the subtraction mirror of the false positive
//    `wql_agg_acc_ty_rule` pins on the way in: a fold that added instead of
//    subtracting answers 9223372036854775818, and one that carried the total in
//    i64 could not represent 2^63 at all. The i64 payload of 2^63 is
//    -9223372036854775808, which is the number the interpreter's `get_i64`
//    hands back for the same total — the same bits, read signed.
//
// C. RETRACTING WHAT WAS NEVER INSERTED IS AN ERROR, AND THE ERROR HAS A NAME
//    THAT DOES NOT LIE. Three distinct mistakes, three assertions, and each one
//    checks WHICH ARM came back rather than merely that something came back —
//    an `Err(_)` wildcard would pass on any arm and the whole point of the arms
//    is that they are different facts:
//
//      • `ElError::RetractAbsent` — a MULTISET-DOMAIN violation. A multiset has
//            no element of negative multiplicity, so a group count or a value
//            multiplicity driven below zero, or a retraction into a group never
//            seen, describes no state the handle can be in. Named for the
//            caller's mistake, not for the arithmetic that noticed.
//      • `ElError::Underflow` — an ARITHMETIC borrow out of a `u64` TOTAL,
//            `el_subu`'s fault and only its. A different mistake with a
//            different repair, so a different name: `Overflow` said "too large"
//            about a number that had gone below zero.
//
//    ⚠⚠ THIS IS WHERE THE STATIC TIER IS STRICTER THAN THE INTERPRETER, AND
//    THE DIVERGENCE IS ASSERTED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. Take `qc` (count only):
//    insert (3,1), retract it, retract it again.
//      static      → `Err(RetractAbsent)` — the count would reach −1.
//      interpreter → `had_error()` FALSE, the group merely absent. Its
//                    `aggstate_ensure` runs before `w`'s sign is read and its
//                    count update is a bare `gcount += w` with NO ERROR PATH AT
//                    ALL — which is not the answer "no error", it is "I was
//                    never asked", so its silence was never agreement.
//    Both answers are pinned below so that the divergence cannot vanish
//    silently while the interpreter is being deleted.
//
//    ⚠ AND THE ORDER OF THE GUARDS IS ITSELF LOAD-BEARING, which is why
//    `el_subu`'s witness had to be REBUILT rather than kept. The group-count
//    guard runs BEFORE the folds, so the old construction — insert (9,5),
//    retract, retract — now returns at the COUNT guard and never reaches
//    `el_subu` at all: the assertion would have stayed green while the witness
//    it was written for evaporated. The witness below keeps the count LEGAL and
//    drives only the SUM under: insert (0,1) twice (count 2, total 2), then
//    retract one row carrying 5 — count 2 → 1 is fine, 2 − 5 in u64 is not.
//
//    ⚠ THE VALUE-MULTIPLICITY guard needs the same care and gets its own query
//    `qm` (count + min + max): insert (0,5) twice, then retract (0,7). The
//    group count goes 2 → 1, which is legal, so the count guard does NOT fire;
//    the map's entry for 7 goes 0 → −1, which is the only thing that can catch
//    it. Without this row that guard is a name no test reads.
//
//    ⚠ AND THROUGH A `FactStore` NONE OF THESE ADDENDS EXIST AT ALL:
//    `retract` returns -1 and pushes nothing when the row is not present, so the
//    store is a SET and a double retraction is a no-op. The cases above are
//    therefore RAW-BATCH cases by construction, which is why they are driven by
//    `<q>_retract` / `<q>_apply` directly and not through a store.
//
// G. THE SOURCE TAG. A delta tagged with a source this query does not HAVE is
//    `Err(ElError::SrcUnknown)`, and the handle is untouched by the refusal.
//    ⚠ THIS ASSERTION WAS INVERTED BY P3 AND THE OLD ONE IS RECORDED HERE SO
//    THE CHANGE IS NOT SILENT: it used to require `Ok(0)`, on the ground that "a
//    delta for a source this operator does not read contributes nothing". That
//    reading is honest only while the tag CANNOT be wrong — one bare scan, so 0
//    is the only tag a caller could mean — and P3 admits a one-step JOIN, which
//    makes a mistyped tag a caller mistake that folds nothing while answering
//    SUCCESS. `qa` still has ONE source, which is exactly why the block belongs
//    here: the strictness is not a property of joins, it is a property of the
//    dispatch, and the bare-scan half of the population is where it would have
//    stayed silent.
//
// H. THE METAMORPHIC LAW, EXTENDED TO SIGNED WEIGHTS: for any split of an
//    insert stream followed by a retraction stream into epochs, the snapshot
//    equals `qa()` over the remaining multiset — same rows, same order.
//
// ── EXIT CODES: THE CEILING IS 124 AND THE PROGRAM REFUSES TO RUN WITHOUT IT ─
// `.expected` says `exit: 0`, so a non-zero code is a DIAGNOSTIC telling a
// reader which assertion fired. 126, 127 and 128+signal are the shell's, so a
// fixture code up there is one number standing for two different states —
// exactly how a crash reads as an assertion, or an assertion as a crash. This
// arc has already paid for that: a candidate's 132 collided with SIGILL and was
// disambiguated only because the harness happened to print the signal's text.
//
// ⚠⚠ AND THIS HEADER WAS ITSELF THE LIE UNTIL 2026-08-07. It said "EVERY ONE
// ≤ 122" while the file emitted 123, 124 AND 125-131 — measured, nine codes, at
// block F4 and block J. 125 is the value the sister fixtures reserve for the
// overrun guard; 126/127 are the shell's; 128-131 are 128+SIGHUP/SIGINT/SIGQUIT/
// SIGILL. Worse, there was NO ceiling machinery of any kind here: no `TOP_CODE`,
// no `check_layout`, no chokepoint on the returned value — the ceiling was a
// sentence, which is the same form as the claim it was written to replace.
//
// ⚠ THE FILE WAS OVER CAPACITY, AND RENUMBERING ALONE COULD NOT FIX IT. Demand
// was 131 states against a hard supply of 124 usable codes, so at least seven
// states had to be SHED, not moved. They were shed where a code was carrying a
// PARAMETER rather than a CLASS: each of F1-F5's `ElError` matches spent three
// codes saying "the refusal came back under the wrong name", which is ONE class
// with the arm as its parameter. Each match now returns one class code and
// PRINTS the arm, freeing ten slots; the seven codes that sat in the guard/shell/
// signal band moved into seven of them (125→79, 126→80, 127→83, 128→84, 129→87,
// 130→88, 131→91) and block J's 124 moved to 92. Block G's four arms had ALREADY
// been collapsed onto code 8 with nothing to tell them apart — one code, four
// states, the defect in its purest form — and they print the arm now too.
// 92 is taken, so 97, 98 and 124 are the only free numbers left: THIS FILE IS AT
// CAPACITY and the next assertion takes the parameter channel, not a number.
//
// The scheme: codes 1-10 are the BLOCKS' PLUMBING (a call that must succeed
// returned `Err` — block A is 1, B is 2, … I is 10), and every genuine
// ASSERTION gets its own code, allocated in source order:
//     A 11-32 · B 33-36 · C 37-42 · D 43-64 · E 65-75 · F 76-93 · 123
//     F5 94-96, 99-105 · G 106-111 · H 112-115 · I 116-122
//     J 79-80, 83-84, 87-88, 91-92 (relocated out of the shell band)
// One code, one CLASS — where a class has parameters they go to stdout, and no
// code can be confused with a signal.
//
// ⚠ 123 IS BLOCK F's AND SITS OUT OF SEQUENCE ON PURPOSE. It is F4's slot-list
// assertion, added after the ranges were allocated; appending it beat shifting
// 47 codes so that one number could be contiguous, and a renumbering is exactly
// the edit that silently invalidates every control measurement taken against
// the old codes. Named here so the gap is a decision and not an oversight.
// ⚠ BLOCK J's CODES DID MOVE, and that is a cost paid knowingly rather than
// avoided: any control revert recorded against 124-131 now lands on 79/80/83/84/
// 87/88/91/92 instead. Leaving them where they were meant leaving four of them
// indistinguishable from a signal, which is the larger lie.
//
// ── AND THE OPPOSITE HOLE, A STATE WITH NO CODE ─────────────────────────────
// All of the above is about ONE CODE STANDING FOR TWO STATES. The inverse ran
// open here too: `.expected` was `exit: 0` with no `stdout:` key, so an
// assertion that STOPPED RUNNING was invisible. `main` now prints
//     three ways ok chk=NNN rows=NNN
// on the success path and `.expected` pins it; both numbers are derived from the
// checks and the snapshots that actually ran. See the census block below.
//
// exit 0 = all three witnesses agree with the table above, under every split.
package test;

use logos.lang.writ.container;
use logos.lang.writ.anyval;
use logos.lang.writ.array;
use logos.std.wql.ir;
use logos.std.wql.catalog_macro;
use logos.std.wql.wql;
use logos.std.wql.el;
use logos.mem.deem;
use logos.lang.result;
use logos.mem.string;
use logos.lang.str;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;
use logos.std.fmt;       // println! — the ceiling guard, the census, and the
                         // PARAMETER channel every shared code now carries

struct S { pub g: i64, pub v: i64 }
struct U { pub g: i64, pub u: u64 }

pub schema SR : code(0x0E45_0000_0000_0011) { g: i64 = 0, v: i64 = 1 }
pub schema UR : code(0x0E45_0000_0000_0012) { g: i64 = 0, u: u64 = 1 }
resource cat = schema_catalog!{ SR, UR };

// count + an i64 sum: both invert exactly, so this query gains the weight.
pub deem qa(ss: &[S]) {
    from ss s group by s.g aggregate c = count(), t = sum(s.v) select (key, c, t)
}
// count + a u64 sum: the other overflow predicate, in both directions.
pub deem qu(us: &[U]) {
    from us s group by s.g aggregate n = count(), t = sum(s.u) select (key, n, t)
}
// count ALONE — the multiplicity on its own, with no sum to err first.
pub deem qc(ss: &[S]) {
    from ss s group by s.g aggregate c = count() select (key, c)
}
// count + min + max — the SEMILATTICE arms, whose state is a value→multiplicity
// MAP rather than a scalar. Present here for one cell only: the map's own
// "retracted a value this group never held" guard, which the group-count guard
// cannot reach. The map's full behaviour (order at 2^63, multiplicity, the
// stale-seed gate, float identity) is `deem_incr_static_retract_e2e`'s subject.
pub deem qm(ss: &[S]) {
    from ss s group by s.g aggregate c = count(), lo = min(s.v), hi = max(s.v) select (key, c, lo, hi)
}

fn qa_text() -> str {
    return "from ss s group by s.g aggregate c = count(), t = sum(s.v) select (key, c, t)";
}
fn qu_text() -> str {
    return "from us s group by s.g aggregate n = count(), t = sum(s.u) select (key, n, t)";
}
fn qc_text() -> str {
    return "from ss s group by s.g aggregate c = count() select (key, c)";
}

// ── the row sets ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
fn ins_rows() -> Vec<S> {
    let mut v: Vec<S> = vec_new::<S>();
    v.push(S { g: 0i64, v:  5i64 });
    v.push(S { g: 1i64, v: 10i64 });
    v.push(S { g: 0i64, v: -3i64 });
    v.push(S { g: 2i64, v: -8i64 });
    v.push(S { g: 0i64, v:  6i64 });
    v.push(S { g: 2i64, v:  2i64 });
    v.push(S { g: 0i64, v:  8i64 });
    return v;
}
fn ret_rows() -> Vec<S> {
    let mut v: Vec<S> = vec_new::<S>();
    v.push(S { g: 0i64, v: -3i64 });
    v.push(S { g: 2i64, v:  2i64 });
    return v;
}
// The multiset that remains — written out BY HAND rather than computed from the
// two lists above, so that the batch witness is not derived from the same
// bookkeeping the incremental one is being checked against.
fn rest_rows() -> Vec<S> {
    let mut v: Vec<S> = vec_new::<S>();
    v.push(S { g: 0i64, v:  5i64 });
    v.push(S { g: 1i64, v: 10i64 });
    v.push(S { g: 2i64, v: -8i64 });
    v.push(S { g: 0i64, v:  6i64 });
    v.push(S { g: 0i64, v:  8i64 });
    return v;
}
// …and what remains after g=1 is emptied too.
fn rest2_rows() -> Vec<S> {
    let mut v: Vec<S> = vec_new::<S>();
    v.push(S { g: 0i64, v:  5i64 });
    v.push(S { g: 2i64, v: -8i64 });
    v.push(S { g: 0i64, v:  6i64 });
    v.push(S { g: 0i64, v:  8i64 });
    return v;
}
fn one_row(g: i64, v: i64) -> Vec<S> {
    let mut o: Vec<S> = vec_new::<S>();
    o.push(S { g: g, v: v });
    return o;
}
fn one_u(g: i64, u: u64) -> Vec<U> {
    let mut o: Vec<U> = vec_new::<U>();
    o.push(U { g: g, u: u });
    return o;
}

fn slice_of(all: &Vec<S>, lo: i64, hi: i64) -> Vec<S> {
    let mut v: Vec<S> = vec_new::<S>();
    let mut i: i64 = lo;
    while i < hi { v.push(all.get(i)); i = i + 1i64; }
    return v;
}

// ── the dynamic side: signed ZBatches over a schema row ────────────────────
fn mkS(d: &Writ, g: i64, v: i64) -> WAny {
    let r: SR = d.make::<SR>(); r.g = g; r.v = v;
    return WAny::ref_to((r.m as i64) as *const u8);
}
fn mkU(d: &Writ, g: i64, u: u64) -> WAny {
    let r: UR = d.make::<UR>(); r.g = g; r.u = u;
    return WAny::ref_to((r.m as i64) as *const u8);
}
fn zb_S(d: &Writ, rs: &Vec<S>, w: i64) -> ZBatch {
    let mut b: ZBatch = ZBatch::new();
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < rs.len() { b.push(mkS(d, rs.get(i).g, rs.get(i).v), w); i = i + 1i64; }
    return b;
}
// The interpreter's snapshot slots are ZOut order, which after a retraction is
// NOT the group's first-occurrence order — so its rows are looked up BY KEY.
// -1 = the key is absent, which is itself an assertable fact (the emptied group).
fn find_key(r: &QRows, k: i64) -> i64 {
    let mut j: i64 = 0i64;
    while j < r.row_count() { if r.get_i64(j, 0i64) == k { return j; } j = j + 1i64; }
    return -1i64;
}

// One static run: the whole insert stream cut into `parts` contiguous epochs,
// then the whole retraction stream cut into `parts` contiguous retractions.
// The ANSWER must not depend on `parts`.
fn incr_in_parts(ins: &Vec<S>, ret: &Vec<S>, parts: i64) -> Result<Vec<(i64, i64, i64)>, ElError> {
    let mut h: QaIncr = qa_incremental();
    let ni: i64 = ins.len();
    let mut p: i64 = 0i64;
    while p < parts {
        let lo: i64 = (ni * p) / parts;
        let hi: i64 = (ni * (p + 1i64)) / parts;
        let ch: Vec<S> = slice_of(ins, lo, hi);
        qa_epoch(&mut h, ch.as_slice())?;
        p = p + 1i64;
    }
    let nr: i64 = ret.len();
    let mut q: i64 = 0i64;
    while q < parts {
        let lo2: i64 = (nr * q) / parts;
        let hi2: i64 = (nr * (q + 1i64)) / parts;
        let ch2: Vec<S> = slice_of(ret, lo2, hi2);
        qa_retract(&mut h, ch2.as_slice())?;
        q = q + 1i64;
    }
    return qa_snapshot(&h);
}

// ── THE CEILING, AS A MECHANISM RATHER THAN A SENTENCE ──────────────────────
// The header used to claim "EVERY ONE ≤ 122" while codes 123-131 were live, and
// nothing in the file refused a 132nd. That is the same form this arc has now
// paid for three times. `TOP_CODE` is a DECLARATION checked before any assertion
// runs, and `main` checks the code `assertions()` ACTUALLY returns against it,
// so an assertion appended past the ceiling is caught on the number and not on
// the declaration.
// PROVEN TO BITE, predicted then reverted: `TOP_CODE` 123 → 130 — a block
// declaring itself into SIGQUIT's number — exits 125 before any assertion runs,
// "declared top 130 exceeds the ceiling 124". The sister file
// `deem_incr_join_e2e` carries the returned-value half of the same proof and the
// control that shows the chokepoint narrows nothing.
fn TOP_CODE()     -> i32 { return 123i32; }
// 124 is the only headroom left, and 97/98 are the only free codes below it —
// see the exit-code block: this file is AT CAPACITY and the next assertion takes
// the parameter channel, it does not take a new number.
fn EXIT_MAX()     -> i32 { return 124i32; }
fn EXIT_OVERRUN() -> i32 { return 125i32; }

fn check_layout() -> i32 {
    if TOP_CODE() > EXIT_MAX() {
        println!("EXIT-CODE LAYOUT: declared top {} exceeds the ceiling {}", TOP_CODE(), EXIT_MAX());
        return EXIT_OVERRUN();
    }
    if EXIT_MAX() >= EXIT_OVERRUN() {
        println!("EXIT-CODE LAYOUT: ceiling {} collides with the guard {}", EXIT_MAX(), EXIT_OVERRUN());
        return EXIT_OVERRUN();
    }
    // ⚠ A HARD 125, NOT `EXIT_OVERRUN()`: this branch fires exactly when
    // `EXIT_OVERRUN` has been edited into the band it exists to stay out of, so
    // reporting through it would report the defect using the defect.
    if EXIT_OVERRUN() > 125i32 {
        println!("EXIT-CODE LAYOUT: guard {} is itself in the shell/signal band", EXIT_OVERRUN());
        return 125i32;
    }
    return 0i32;
}

// ── THE CENSUS: A STATE WITH NO CODE ────────────────────────────────────────
// `.expected` was `exit: 0` with NO `stdout:` key, and `run_test.sh:217`
// compares stdout only when the key is present — so an assertion that STOPPED
// RUNNING was invisible: delete one and the file still exits 0 with nothing
// moved. The ceiling above is about ONE CODE STANDING FOR TWO STATES; this is
// the inverse, and it was open here for the same reason it was open in
// `deem_incr_static_retract_e2e`.
//
// `chk` counts ASSERTION SITES THAT EXECUTED — bumped inside `bad`, the
// chokepoint all 100 predicates below pass through, and inside `arm_ok` for the
// six refusal blocks whose assertion is a `match` and not an `if`. `rows` counts
// the rows each snapshot and each batch recompute actually handed back. Two axes
// of different kind on purpose: `chk` counts SYNTAX that ran and `rows` counts
// DATA that came back, so a deleted predicate moves one and a deleted BLOCK
// moves both.
//
// ⚠ NEITHER IS A STRAIGHT-LINE CONSTANT HERE, which is what makes this file the
// most informative of the three censuses: block H re-runs the whole insert +
// retract stream at every SPLIT (`parts` = 1..n) and asserts inside the loop, so
// a split that stops being tried lowers `chk` and `rows` even though every `if`
// is still in the source — the failure the exit code cannot carry at all,
// because the run still exits 0.
// PINNED: `three ways ok chk=187 rows=55`. HAND-DERIVED FIRST AND MEASURED
// SECOND, and they agreed to the unit: 187 = 87 straight-line predicates + 94
// loop-executed ones (B 3·3, C 3·3, D 3·2, H 1·7 + 3·3·7) + 6 refusal arms;
// 55 = the rows the 23 snapshot and batch sites actually returned, `sv`'s three
// counted seven times and `w1`'s emptied group counted as zero. A disagreement
// would have been evidence about the code, not a licence to write down whatever
// printed.
// PROVEN TO BITE, predicted then reverted: delete the innermost metamorphic
// assertion `sv.get(z).2 != b1.get(z).2` in block H and the run still exits 0
// but prints `chk=166` — TWENTY-ONE lower for ONE deleted line, because that
// line is checked once per group per split. `run_test.sh` says `FAIL: stdout
// mismatch` and a deletion that used to be invisible is red.
// ⚠ WHAT THIS DOES NOT COVER, NAMED AT THE MECHANISM RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE
// FOUND: a census sees an assertion that stops RUNNING, not one WEAKENED IN
// PLACE. Rewriting a comparison into a tautology still calls the chokepoint once
// and still reads the same rows, so `chk` and `rows` are unmoved — MEASURED
// 2026-08-07 on the sister file, where `f64_to_bits(x) != f64_to_bits(x)` passed
// green with the census at its full count. That failure belongs to review, not
// to this counter.
// ⚠ THIS PARAGRAPH WAS MISSING HERE while the sister file carried it and the
// slice's report claimed all three files did. Two of the three census fixtures
// documented a blind spot the third did not — added 2026-08-07. A limitation
// recorded in one file of a family reads, from the other two, as no limitation.
struct Tally { pub chk: i64, pub rows: i64 }

fn bad(t: &mut Tally, c: bool) -> bool { t.chk = t.chk + 1i64; return c; }
fn arm_ok(t: &mut Tally) { t.chk = t.chk + 1i64; }

fn main() -> i32 {
    let lay: i32 = check_layout();
    if lay != 0i32 { return lay; }
    let mut t: Tally = Tally { chk: 0i64, rows: 0i64 };
    let r: i32 = assertions(&mut t);
    if r > TOP_CODE() {
        println!("EXIT-CODE OVERRUN: an assertion returned {} > TOP_CODE {}", r, TOP_CODE());
        return EXIT_OVERRUN();
    }
    // THE CENSUS, AND IT IS AN ASSERTION: `.expected` pins this line. Printed
    // only on the success path, so a failing run says on stdout what its shared
    // code cannot (the WRONG ARM lines above) and nothing else.
    if r == 0i32 { println!("three ways ok chk={} rows={}", t.chk, t.rows); }
    return r;
}

fn assertions(t: &mut Tally) -> i32 {
    let c: SchemaCatalog = cat();
    let ins: Vec<S> = ins_rows();
    let ret: Vec<S> = ret_rows();
    let rest: Vec<S> = rest_rows();
    let rest2: Vec<S> = rest2_rows();

    // ══ A. THE HAND TABLE, against the static handle ═══════════════════════
    let mut h: QaIncr = qa_incremental();
    let ni: i64 = match qa_epoch(&mut h, ins.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(n) => { n } Result::Err(_e) => { return 1i32; }
    };
    if bad(t, ni != 7i64) { return 11i32; }
    // …insert-only first, so a defect in the RETRACTION cannot hide behind a
    // defect in the fold it inverts.
    let a0: Vec<(i64, i64, i64)> = match qa_snapshot(&h) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 1i32; }
    };
    t.rows = t.rows + a0.len();
    if bad(t, a0.len() != 3i64) { return 12i32; }
    if bad(t, a0.get(0i64).0 !=  0i64) { return 13i32; }
    if bad(t, a0.get(0i64).1 !=  4i64) { return 14i32; }
    if bad(t, a0.get(0i64).2 != 16i64) { return 15i32; }
    if bad(t, a0.get(1i64).0 !=  1i64) { return 16i32; }
    if bad(t, a0.get(1i64).1 !=  1i64) { return 17i32; }
    if bad(t, a0.get(1i64).2 != 10i64) { return 18i32; }
    if bad(t, a0.get(2i64).0 !=  2i64) { return 19i32; }
    if bad(t, a0.get(2i64).1 !=  2i64) { return 20i32; }
    if bad(t, a0.get(2i64).2 != -6i64) { return 21i32; }

    let nr: i64 = match qa_retract(&mut h, ret.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(n) => { n } Result::Err(_e) => { return 1i32; }
    };
    if bad(t, nr != 2i64) { return 22i32; }
    let a1: Vec<(i64, i64, i64)> = match qa_snapshot(&h) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 1i32; }
    };
    t.rows = t.rows + a1.len();
    if bad(t, a1.len() != 3i64) { return 23i32; }
    if bad(t, a1.get(0i64).0 !=  0i64) { return 24i32; }
    if bad(t, a1.get(0i64).1 !=  3i64) { return 25i32; }
    if bad(t, a1.get(0i64).2 != 19i64) { return 26i32; }
    if bad(t, a1.get(1i64).0 !=  1i64) { return 27i32; }
    if bad(t, a1.get(1i64).1 !=  1i64) { return 28i32; }
    if bad(t, a1.get(1i64).2 != 10i64) { return 29i32; }
    if bad(t, a1.get(2i64).0 !=  2i64) { return 30i32; }
    if bad(t, a1.get(2i64).1 !=  1i64) { return 31i32; }
    if bad(t, a1.get(2i64).2 != -8i64) { return 32i32; }

    // ══ B. against the static BATCH fn over the REMAINING multiset ═════════
    // This is the correctness spec, not a second opinion: a handle that has seen
    // I then R must answer what a batch over I∖R answers, element by element.
    let b1: Vec<(i64, i64, i64)> = match qa(rest.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 2i32; }
    };
    t.rows = t.rows + b1.len();
    if bad(t, b1.len() != a1.len()) { return 33i32; }
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < b1.len() {
        if bad(t, b1.get(i).0 != a1.get(i).0) { return 34i32; }
        if bad(t, b1.get(i).1 != a1.get(i).1) { return 35i32; }
        if bad(t, b1.get(i).2 != a1.get(i).2) { return 36i32; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }

    // ══ C. against the DYNAMIC interpreter, driven by a signed ZBatch ══════
    // ⚠ THE INSERTS ARRIVE THROUGH THE BASE-FILL AND NOT THROUGH AN `epoch`
    // OVER AN EMPTY BINDING, AND THAT IS A MEASURED CONSTRAINT ON THE ORACLE
    // RATHER THAN A STYLE CHOICE. `Query::compile` + `incremental` take the
    // column's TYPE FACTS from the bound source's rows — with an empty array
    // bound, `QPlan.agg_argu` comes back false for a `u64` column and the
    // engine folds it under the SIGNED predicate: measured here as
    // `max(u64){5, 2^63}` answering 5, and `sum(u64){2^62, 2^62}` latching an
    // overflow and answering 2^62. Bind the rows, let construction fold them,
    // and send only the RETRACTIONS through `epoch` — which is what a
    // retraction test wants anyway, since the insert direction is already
    // pinned by `wql_incr_static_three_ways`.
    let d: Writ = writ_new(65536i64);
    let ins_arr: &mut WArray<WAny> = d.array(ins.len() + 1i64);
    let mut ai0: i64 = 0i64;
    while ai0 < ins.len() { ins_arr.push(mkS(&d, ins.get(ai0).g, ins.get(ai0).v)); ai0 = ai0 + 1i64; }
    let mut e: QEnv = QEnv::new();
    e.bind_source("ss", WAny::ref_to(ins_arr as *const WArray<WAny> as *const u8));
    let dq: Query = match Query::compile(qa_text(), &c) {
        Result::Ok(qq) => { qq } Result::Err(_e) => { return 3i32; }
    };
    let mut eng: IncrJoin = match dq.incremental(&e) {
        Result::Ok(g) => { g } Result::Err(_e) => { return 3i32; }
    };
    let nothing: ZBatch = ZBatch::new();
    if bad(t, eng.had_error()) { return 37i32; }
    let dre: ZBatch = zb_S(&d, &ret, 0i64 - 1i64);
    eng.epoch(&dre, &nothing);
    if bad(t, eng.had_error()) { return 38i32; }
    let dr: QRows = eng.snapshot();
    t.rows = t.rows + dr.row_count();
    if bad(t, dr.row_count() != 3i64) { return 39i32; }
    let mut k: i64 = 0i64;
    while k < 3i64 {
        let key: i64 = a1.get(k).0;
        let j: i64 = find_key(&dr, key);
        if bad(t, j < 0i64) { return 40i32; }
        if bad(t, dr.get_i64(j, 1i64) != a1.get(k).1) { return 41i32; }
        if bad(t, dr.get_i64(j, 2i64) != a1.get(k).2) { return 42i32; }
        k = k + 1i64;
    }

    // ══ D. THE EMPTIED GROUP — absent, order of the survivors unchanged ════
    let g1: Vec<S> = one_row(1i64, 10i64);
    match qa_retract(&mut h, g1.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 4i32; }
    }
    let a2: Vec<(i64, i64, i64)> = match qa_snapshot(&h) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 4i32; }
    };
    t.rows = t.rows + a2.len();
    if bad(t, a2.len() != 2i64) { return 43i32; }
    if bad(t, a2.get(0i64).0 !=  0i64) { return 44i32; }
    if bad(t, a2.get(0i64).1 !=  3i64) { return 45i32; }
    if bad(t, a2.get(0i64).2 != 19i64) { return 46i32; }
    if bad(t, a2.get(1i64).0 !=  2i64) { return 47i32; }
    if bad(t, a2.get(1i64).1 !=  1i64) { return 48i32; }
    if bad(t, a2.get(1i64).2 != -8i64) { return 49i32; }
    // …and that IS what the batch answers over what remains.
    let b2: Vec<(i64, i64, i64)> = match qa(rest2.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 4i32; }
    };
    t.rows = t.rows + b2.len();
    if bad(t, b2.len() != a2.len()) { return 50i32; }
    let mut m: i64 = 0i64;
    while m < b2.len() {
        if bad(t, b2.get(m).0 != a2.get(m).0) { return 51i32; }
        if bad(t, b2.get(m).1 != a2.get(m).1) { return 52i32; }
        if bad(t, b2.get(m).2 != a2.get(m).2) { return 53i32; }
        m = m + 1i64;
    }
    // The interpreter drops the same row, for its own reason (`live = false`).
    let dg1: ZBatch = zb_S(&d, &g1, 0i64 - 1i64);
    eng.epoch(&dg1, &nothing);
    if bad(t, eng.had_error()) { return 54i32; }
    let dr2: QRows = eng.snapshot();
    t.rows = t.rows + dr2.row_count();
    if bad(t, dr2.row_count() != 2i64) { return 55i32; }
    if bad(t, find_key(&dr2, 1i64) >= 0i64) { return 56i32; }
    if bad(t, find_key(&dr2, 0i64) < 0i64) { return 57i32; }
    if bad(t, find_key(&dr2, 2i64) < 0i64) { return 58i32; }

    // …AND THE TOMBSTONE IS A TOMBSTONE: re-inserting the key brings the group
    // back in its ORIGINAL position, because the slot was kept rather than
    // deleted. A `__gk` entry that had been removed would put g=1 last.
    match qa_epoch(&mut h, g1.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 4i32; }
    }
    let a3: Vec<(i64, i64, i64)> = match qa_snapshot(&h) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 4i32; }
    };
    t.rows = t.rows + a3.len();
    if bad(t, a3.len() != 3i64) { return 59i32; }
    if bad(t, a3.get(0i64).0 != 0i64) { return 60i32; }
    if bad(t, a3.get(1i64).0 != 1i64) { return 61i32; }
    if bad(t, a3.get(1i64).1 != 1i64) { return 62i32; }
    if bad(t, a3.get(1i64).2 != 10i64) { return 63i32; }
    if bad(t, a3.get(2i64).0 != 2i64) { return 64i32; }

    // ══ E. THE u64 EXTREME — 2^63 EXACTLY, in the subtract direction ═══════
    let mut hu: QuIncr = qu_incremental();
    let two63: u64 = 9223372036854775808u64;
    let uins: Vec<U> = { let mut v: Vec<U> = vec_new::<U>();
                         v.push(U { g: 0i64, u: two63 });
                         v.push(U { g: 0i64, u: 5u64 });
                         v };
    match qu_epoch(&mut hu, uins.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 5i32; }
    }
    let u0: Vec<(i64, i64, u64)> = match qu_snapshot(&hu) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 5i32; }
    };
    t.rows = t.rows + u0.len();
    if bad(t, u0.len() != 1i64) { return 65i32; }
    if bad(t, u0.get(0i64).1 != 2i64) { return 66i32; }
    if bad(t, u0.get(0i64).2 != 9223372036854775813u64) { return 67i32; }
    let ufive: Vec<U> = one_u(0i64, 5u64);
    match qu_retract(&mut hu, ufive.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 5i32; }
    }
    let u1: Vec<(i64, i64, u64)> = match qu_snapshot(&hu) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 5i32; }
    };
    t.rows = t.rows + u1.len();
    if bad(t, u1.len() != 1i64) { return 68i32; }
    if bad(t, u1.get(0i64).1 != 1i64) { return 69i32; }
    if bad(t, u1.get(0i64).2 != two63) { return 70i32; }
    // …and the interpreter's total is the SAME BITS, read signed. Same
    // constraint as block C: the two rows are BOUND, so construction folds them
    // and the `u64` claim survives into the plan.
    let ud: Writ = writ_new(65536i64);
    let uarr: &mut WArray<WAny> = ud.array(4i64);
    uarr.push(mkU(&ud, 0i64, two63));
    uarr.push(mkU(&ud, 0i64, 5u64));
    let mut ue: QEnv = QEnv::new();
    ue.bind_source("us", WAny::ref_to(uarr as *const WArray<WAny> as *const u8));
    let uqq: Query = match Query::compile(qu_text(), &c) {
        Result::Ok(qq) => { qq } Result::Err(_e) => { return 5i32; }
    };
    let mut ueng: IncrJoin = match uqq.incremental(&ue) {
        Result::Ok(g) => { g } Result::Err(_e) => { return 5i32; }
    };
    if bad(t, ueng.had_error()) { return 71i32; }
    let mut ur: ZBatch = ZBatch::new();
    ur.push(mkU(&ud, 0i64, 5u64), 0i64 - 1i64);
    ueng.epoch(&ur, &nothing);
    if bad(t, ueng.had_error()) { return 72i32; }
    let urows: QRows = ueng.snapshot();
    t.rows = t.rows + urows.row_count();
    if bad(t, urows.row_count() != 1i64) { return 73i32; }
    if bad(t, urows.get_i64(0i64, 1i64) != 1i64) { return 74i32; }
    // 2^63 as an i64 payload — `-9223372036854775808`, written out rather than
    // computed, which is the spelling the corpus already pins elsewhere.
    if bad(t, urows.get_i64(0i64, 2i64) != -9223372036854775808i64) { return 75i32; }

    // ══ F. RETRACTING WHAT WAS NEVER INSERTED — THREE MISTAKES, THREE NAMES ═
    //
    // F1. THE GROUP COUNT BELOW ZERO. Insert (9,5), retract it, retract it
    // again: the group's multiplicity would reach −1, a state no multiset has.
    let mut hz: QuIncr = qu_incremental();
    let z5: Vec<U> = one_u(9i64, 5u64);
    match qu_epoch(&mut hz, z5.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 6i32; }
    }
    match qu_retract(&mut hz, z5.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 6i32; }
    }
    // the group is empty now — and absent
    let z0: Vec<(i64, i64, u64)> = match qu_snapshot(&hz) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 6i32; }
    };
    t.rows = t.rows + z0.len();
    if bad(t, z0.len() != 0i64) { return 76i32; }
    // …and the THIRD addend must say WHICH mistake, by name.
    match qu_retract(&mut hz, z5.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { return 77i32; }
        Result::Err(e1) => {
            match e1 {
                ElError::RetractAbsent => { arm_ok(t); }
                // `Underflow` here would mean the count guard had been removed
                // and `el_subu` caught the same call instead — a different
                // fact reported for the same input.
                ElError::Underflow  => { println!("F1 WRONG ARM: Underflow — the count guard was removed and `el_subu` caught the same call instead"); return 78i32; }
                ElError::Overflow   => { println!("F1 WRONG ARM: Overflow"); return 78i32; }
                ElError::DivByZero  => { println!("F1 WRONG ARM: DivByZero"); return 78i32; }
                // ⚠ `SrcUnknown` IS THIS BLOCK'S PLUMBING, NOT A NEW ASSERTION,
                // AND THAT IS WHY IT REUSES CODE 6 RATHER THAN TAKING A NEW ONE.
                // The tag reaching `<q>_apply` here is the LITERAL `0i32` the
                // forwarder writes, so a source fault means the dispatch is not
                // reading its own parameter — "a call that must succeed
                // returned Err", which is exactly what codes 1-10 are for.
                // ⚠⚠ AND THE FIXTURE STILL HAS NO ROOM FOR A NEW CODE. When this
                // note was written, block J had already appended 126-131 into the
                // shell/signal band; those are relocated now (see the exit-code
                // block) but the space did not grow — 97, 98 and 124 are the only
                // free numbers under the ceiling. Anything wanting more than a
                // class here takes the PARAMETER channel: a `println!`, which is
                // what F1-F5's wrong arms and block G's four arms now do.
                ElError::SrcUnknown => { return 6i32; }
            }
        }
    }

    // F2. A VALUE THIS GROUP NEVER HELD, ON A QUERY WITH NO VALUE MAP.
    //
    // ⚠⚠ THIS BLOCK CHANGED ARM ON 2026-08-08. It read "THE u64 TOTAL BELOW ZERO
    // — `el_subu`'s OWN witness": insert (7,1) twice (count 2, total 2), then
    // retract ONE row carrying 5, so the count 2 → 1 stays legal and 2 − 5 in
    // u64 is the borrow. `<q>_apply` now carries a FOOTPRINT sensor on the bare
    // scan (`emit_incremental`) — the handle keeps the (key, aggregate-argument)
    // tuples it has actually been handed, in Z-set normal form, and refuses a
    // retraction of one it does not hold BEFORE any fold — so `(7,5)`, never
    // inserted, is `RetractAbsent`. That is the right answer for this input: it
    // names the caller's mistake instead of reporting a borrow that only
    // happened because the mistake went undetected.
    //
    // ⚠ AND `el_subu`'s BORROW IS NOW UNREACHABLE THROUGH `<q>_retract`, WHICH
    // IS A PROOF AND NOT AN OBSERVATION: every stored weight is ≥ 0, so a
    // group's u64 total is Σ_f w_f·v_f, a retraction is admitted only for a
    // footprint f with |w| ≤ w_f, and it subtracts |w|·v_f ≤ w_f·v_f ≤ total.
    // The subtrahend is bounded by one term of the sum it comes out of.
    // ⚠ SO THE ARITHMETIC WITNESS MOVED TO ITS OWN SEAM AND IS NOT LOST:
    // `deem_incr_static_retract_e2e` block E now calls `el_subu(2,5)` directly
    // and asserts `Underflow`, plus `el_subu(5,2) == 3` as the control. Those
    // two blocks were `el_subu`'s ONLY witnesses in the corpus; deleting them
    // would have left a guard against a u64 wrap (measured elsewhere in this
    // file as an "Illegal instruction") asserted by nothing at all.
    let mut hb2: QuIncr = qu_incremental();
    let b1u: Vec<U> = { let mut o: Vec<U> = vec_new::<U>();
                        o.push(U { g: 7i64, u: 1u64 });
                        o.push(U { g: 7i64, u: 1u64 }); o };
    match qu_epoch(&mut hb2, b1u.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 6i32; }
    }
    let b5u: Vec<U> = one_u(7i64, 5u64);
    match qu_retract(&mut hb2, b5u.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { return 81i32; }
        Result::Err(e2) => {
            match e2 {
                ElError::RetractAbsent => { arm_ok(t); }
                // `Underflow` here means the FOOTPRINT SENSOR is gone and
                // `el_subu` caught the same call instead — a different fact
                // reported for the same input, and the shape of the regression
                // this block now guards.
                ElError::Underflow => { println!("F2 WRONG ARM: Underflow — the footprint sensor is gone and el_subu caught the same call"); return 82i32; }
                ElError::Overflow      => { println!("F2 WRONG ARM: Overflow"); return 82i32; }
                ElError::DivByZero     => { println!("F2 WRONG ARM: DivByZero"); return 82i32; }
                ElError::SrcUnknown    => { return 6i32; }   // plumbing — see F1
            }
        }
    }

    // F3. A VALUE'S MULTIPLICITY BELOW ZERO. Insert (0,5) twice (count 2, map
    // {5:2}), then retract (0,7): count 2 → 1 is fine, and 7 is a value this
    // group never held. A `remove` on a missing key is a silent no-op, so
    // WITHOUT a guard this returns Ok and the retraction simply vanishes.
    //
    // ⚠ THE ARM IS UNCHANGED AND THE MECHANISM BEHIND IT IS NOT, WHICH IS
    // EXACTLY THE KIND OF DRIFT A COMMENT HIDES. Until 2026-08-08 this was the
    // MAP's own guard (`__nc < 0` inside the min/max fold element). The
    // footprint sensor in `<q>_apply` now answers first, for every bare-scan
    // query, and returns the SAME `RetractAbsent` — so the map guard is no
    // longer reachable from `<q>_retract` on this shape. It stays as a floor,
    // and it is what still answers on the JOIN path, where the sensor's identity
    // is over the delta side only. Nothing here asserts the map guard any more;
    // what this block asserts is the NAME the surface gives the mistake.
    let mut hm: QmIncr = qm_incremental();
    let m5: Vec<S> = { let mut o: Vec<S> = vec_new::<S>();
                       o.push(S { g: 0i64, v: 5i64 });
                       o.push(S { g: 0i64, v: 5i64 }); o };
    match qm_epoch(&mut hm, m5.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 6i32; }
    }
    let m7: Vec<S> = one_row(0i64, 7i64);
    match qm_retract(&mut hm, m7.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { return 85i32; }
        Result::Err(e3) => {
            match e3 {
                ElError::RetractAbsent => { arm_ok(t); }
                ElError::Underflow  => { println!("F3 WRONG ARM: Underflow — the group-count guard answered before the map's"); return 86i32; }
                ElError::Overflow   => { println!("F3 WRONG ARM: Overflow"); return 86i32; }
                ElError::DivByZero  => { println!("F3 WRONG ARM: DivByZero"); return 86i32; }
                ElError::SrcUnknown => { return 6i32; }   // plumbing — see F1
            }
        }
    }

    // F4. A GROUP NEVER SEEN AT ALL. The retraction names a key with no slot,
    // so no group is created for it either — a `+0`-weight phantom slot is
    // exactly what the tombstone list must not accumulate.
    let mut hn: QcIncr = qc_incremental();
    let n9: Vec<S> = one_row(9i64, 1i64);
    match qc_retract(&mut hn, n9.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { return 89i32; }
        Result::Err(e4) => {
            match e4 {
                ElError::RetractAbsent => { arm_ok(t); }
                ElError::Underflow  => { println!("F4 WRONG ARM: Underflow — an absent GROUP was reported as an arithmetic borrow"); return 90i32; }
                ElError::Overflow   => { println!("F4 WRONG ARM: Overflow"); return 90i32; }
                ElError::DivByZero  => { println!("F4 WRONG ARM: DivByZero"); return 90i32; }
                ElError::SrcUnknown => { return 6i32; }   // plumbing — see F1
            }
        }
    }
    // …and the handle is still EMPTY: the refused retraction created nothing.
    let nz: Vec<(i64, i64)> = match qc_snapshot(&hn) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 6i32; }
    };
    t.rows = t.rows + nz.len();
    if bad(t, nz.len() != 0i64) { return 93i32; }
    // ⚠ AND THE SNAPSHOT ALONE CANNOT SAY THAT — WHICH IS WHY THIS LINE REACHES
    // INTO THE HANDLE. `<q>_snapshot` skips any group whose count is not
    // positive, so a PHANTOM slot created by the refused retraction (key 9,
    // count −1, seeds run, maps allocated) is invisible to it and `nz.len()`
    // reads 0 either way. Without the group-creation guard the slot list GROWS
    // on every refused retraction — unbounded under P3's fixpoint, which retries
    // over exactly this state — so the assertion has to be about `__gk` itself.
    // The handle's fields are `pub` by construction; this is the one place in
    // the corpus that depends on that, deliberately.
    if bad(t, hn.__gk.len() != 0i64) { return 123i32; }

    // ══ F5. THE DIVERGENCE, ASSERTED FROM BOTH SIDES ═══════════════════════
    // The count-only triple: static `Err(RetractAbsent)`, interpreter silent.
    // Neither half of this may be dropped — the static answer alone would look
    // like agreement, and the interpreter answer alone would look like a bug.
    let mut hc: QcIncr = qc_incremental();
    let c5: Vec<S> = one_row(3i64, 1i64);
    match qc_epoch(&mut hc, c5.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 7i32; }
    }
    match qc_retract(&mut hc, c5.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 7i32; }
    }
    // the group is empty and absent — both tiers agree about THAT
    let cz: Vec<(i64, i64)> = match qc_snapshot(&hc) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 7i32; }
    };
    t.rows = t.rows + cz.len();
    if bad(t, cz.len() != 0i64) { return 94i32; }
    // …and here they part. STATIC: strict.
    match qc_retract(&mut hc, c5.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { return 95i32; }
        Result::Err(e5) => {
            match e5 {
                ElError::RetractAbsent => { arm_ok(t); }
                ElError::Underflow  => { println!("F5 WRONG ARM: Underflow"); return 96i32; }
                ElError::Overflow   => { println!("F5 WRONG ARM: Overflow"); return 96i32; }
                ElError::DivByZero  => { println!("F5 WRONG ARM: DivByZero"); return 96i32; }
                ElError::SrcUnknown => { return 7i32; }   // plumbing — see F1 (F5's code)
            }
        }
    }
    // INTERPRETER: the same triple over a count-only query, and it does NOT
    // report. Bound to the single row (3,1) so construction folds it, then the
    // SAME retraction twice through `epoch`.
    let carr: &mut WArray<WAny> = d.array(4i64);
    carr.push(mkS(&d, 3i64, 1i64));
    let mut ce: QEnv = QEnv::new();
    ce.bind_source("ss", WAny::ref_to(carr as *const WArray<WAny> as *const u8));
    let cqq: Query = match Query::compile(qc_text(), &c) {
        Result::Ok(qq) => { qq } Result::Err(_e) => { return 7i32; }
    };
    let mut ceng: IncrJoin = match cqq.incremental(&ce) {
        Result::Ok(g) => { g } Result::Err(_e) => { return 7i32; }
    };
    if bad(t, ceng.had_error()) { return 99i32; }
    let mut cr: ZBatch = ZBatch::new();
    cr.push(mkS(&d, 3i64, 1i64), 0i64 - 1i64);
    ceng.epoch(&cr, &nothing);
    if bad(t, ceng.had_error()) { return 100i32; }
    ceng.epoch(&cr, &nothing);
    // ⚠ THE DIVERGENCE, PINNED. `had_error()` FALSE where the static tier
    // returned `RetractAbsent`. If a later slice makes the interpreter strict,
    // or makes the static tier lax, THIS line goes red and says so — which is
    // the whole reason it is an assertion and not a comment.
    if bad(t, ceng.had_error()) { return 101i32; }
    if bad(t, ceng.snapshot().row_count() != 0i64) { return 102i32; }

    // F6. …AND THE u64 SUM'S FAULT IS THE ONE CELL WHERE THE TWO TIERS STILL
    // AGREE THAT SOMETHING IS WRONG, by different routes. The interpreter has
    // no count guard, so its `el_wrap_sub(0, 5)` → `None` latches `math_err` on
    // exactly the addend where the static tier's F1 count guard returned first.
    // Same input, same verdict "error", different reason — asserted so that the
    // agreement is not mistaken for the two tiers running the same check.
    let zarr: &mut WArray<WAny> = ud.array(4i64);
    zarr.push(mkU(&ud, 9i64, 5u64));
    let mut ze: QEnv = QEnv::new();
    ze.bind_source("us", WAny::ref_to(zarr as *const WArray<WAny> as *const u8));
    let mut zeng: IncrJoin = match uqq.incremental(&ze) {
        Result::Ok(g) => { g } Result::Err(_e) => { return 7i32; }
    };
    if bad(t, zeng.had_error()) { return 103i32; }
    let mut zr: ZBatch = ZBatch::new();
    zr.push(mkU(&ud, 9i64, 5u64), 0i64 - 1i64);
    zeng.epoch(&zr, &nothing);
    if bad(t, zeng.had_error()) { return 104i32; }
    zeng.epoch(&zr, &nothing);
    if bad(t, !zeng.had_error()) { return 105i32; }

    // ══ G. THE SOURCE TAG — a tag this query has no source for ═════════════
    //
    // ⚠⚠ THIS BLOCK ASSERTED THE OPPOSITE UNTIL P3, AND THE INVERSION IS THE
    // POINT. It used to require `qa_apply(&h, 3i32, −1, …)` to answer `Ok(0)`,
    // under the ground "a delta for a source this operator does not read
    // contributes nothing". That is true of a tag that CANNOT be wrong — while
    // `incr_eligible` admitted exactly one bare scan, 0 was the only tag any
    // caller could mean — and it becomes a silent success that folds nothing the
    // moment a query has two relations to name. P3 admits a join, so the tag is
    // now live and STRICT: out of `0 ..= ch.n` is `Err(ElError::SrcUnknown)`.
    // `qa` has ONE source, so 3 names nothing and the call must be refused.
    //
    // ⚠ THE HANDLE IS UNTOUCHED BY THE REFUSAL, and that is asserted below
    // rather than assumed. The dispatch runs BEFORE any fold, so a rejected tag
    // is one of the few `<q>_apply` errors that leaves the handle in a
    // SPECIFIED state — worth pinning precisely because the fn's contract says
    // the opposite about faults raised mid-fold.
    let mut ht: QaIncr = qa_incremental();
    match qa_epoch(&mut ht, ins.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 8i32; }
    }
    match qa_apply(&mut ht, 3i32, 0i64 - 1i64, ret.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { return 106i32; }
        Result::Err(eg) => {
            match eg {
                ElError::SrcUnknown    => { arm_ok(t); }
                ElError::RetractAbsent => { println!("G WRONG ARM: RetractAbsent — an out-of-range TAG was reported as an absent row"); return 8i32; }
                ElError::Underflow     => { println!("G WRONG ARM: Underflow"); return 8i32; }
                ElError::Overflow      => { println!("G WRONG ARM: Overflow"); return 8i32; }
                ElError::DivByZero     => { println!("G WRONG ARM: DivByZero"); return 8i32; }
            }
        }
    }
    let t0: Vec<(i64, i64, i64)> = match qa_snapshot(&ht) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 8i32; }
    };
    t.rows = t.rows + t0.len();
    if bad(t, t0.len() != 3i64) { return 107i32; }
    if bad(t, t0.get(0i64).2 != 16i64) { return 108i32; }   // untouched by the tagged delta
    // …and tag 0 through `_apply` is exactly what `_retract` forwards.
    let ntag: i64 = match qa_apply(&mut ht, 0i32, 0i64 - 1i64, ret.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(n) => { n } Result::Err(_e) => { return 8i32; }
    };
    if bad(t, ntag != 2i64) { return 109i32; }
    let t1: Vec<(i64, i64, i64)> = match qa_snapshot(&ht) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 8i32; }
    };
    t.rows = t.rows + t1.len();
    if bad(t, t1.len() != 3i64) { return 110i32; }
    if bad(t, t1.get(0i64).2 != 19i64) { return 111i32; }

    // ══ H. THE METAMORPHIC LAW UNDER SIGNED WEIGHTS ════════════════════════
    // The answer does not depend on how the insert stream or the retraction
    // stream is cut into epochs.
    let mut parts: i64 = 1i64;
    while parts <= 7i64 {
        let sv: Vec<(i64, i64, i64)> = match incr_in_parts(&ins, &ret, parts) {
            Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 9i32; }
        };
        t.rows = t.rows + sv.len();
        if bad(t, sv.len() != b1.len()) { return 112i32; }
        let mut z: i64 = 0i64;
        while z < b1.len() {
            if bad(t, sv.get(z).0 != b1.get(z).0) { return 113i32; }
            if bad(t, sv.get(z).1 != b1.get(z).1) { return 114i32; }
            if bad(t, sv.get(z).2 != b1.get(z).2) { return 115i32; }
            z = z + 1i64;
        }
        parts = parts + 1i64;
    }

    // ══ I. A WEIGHT OF |w| > 1 IS |w| COPIES, IN BOTH DIRECTIONS ═══════════
    // The Z-set reading of the weight, asserted rather than described: +3 over
    // one row and then -3 over it returns the handle to where it started.
    let mut hw: QaIncr = qa_incremental();
    let r1: Vec<S> = one_row(4i64, 7i64);
    match qa_apply(&mut hw, 0i32, 3i64, r1.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 10i32; }
    }
    let w0: Vec<(i64, i64, i64)> = match qa_snapshot(&hw) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 10i32; }
    };
    t.rows = t.rows + w0.len();
    if bad(t, w0.len() != 1i64) { return 116i32; }
    if bad(t, w0.get(0i64).1 !=  3i64) { return 117i32; }
    if bad(t, w0.get(0i64).2 != 21i64) { return 118i32; }
    match qa_apply(&mut hw, 0i32, 0i64 - 3i64, r1.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 10i32; }
    }
    let w1: Vec<(i64, i64, i64)> = match qa_snapshot(&hw) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 10i32; }
    };
    t.rows = t.rows + w1.len();
    if bad(t, w1.len() != 0i64) { return 119i32; }
    // …and the u64 side takes the magnitude through its own predicate.
    let mut hu2: QuIncr = qu_incremental();
    let uw: Vec<U> = one_u(1i64, 3u64);
    match qu_apply(&mut hu2, 0i32, 4i64, uw.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 10i32; }
    }
    let v0: Vec<(i64, i64, u64)> = match qu_snapshot(&hu2) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 10i32; }
    };
    t.rows = t.rows + v0.len();
    if bad(t, v0.len() != 1i64) { return 120i32; }
    if bad(t, v0.get(0i64).2 != 12u64) { return 121i32; }
    match qu_apply(&mut hu2, 0i32, 0i64 - 4i64, uw.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 10i32; }
    }
    let v1: Vec<(i64, i64, u64)> = match qu_snapshot(&hu2) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 10i32; }
    };
    t.rows = t.rows + v1.len();
    if bad(t, v1.len() != 0i64) { return 122i32; }

    // ── J. |w| > 1 THROUGH THE SEMILATTICE MAP — a PARTIAL retraction ───────
    // The blocks above drive |w| > 1 through `count` and `sum` only, whose
    // state is a SCALAR. min/max keep a value→multiplicity MAP, and until this
    // block NOTHING asked whether that map reads the weight or assumes ±1:
    // hard-coding the map's `__cur + __w` back to ±1 left the whole 322-test
    // arc subset GREEN. That is the shape this tree keeps closing — a claim in
    // a comment that no gate pins — so the discriminating cell lives here.
    //
    // The cell is a retraction the entry must SURVIVE. A weight that is read
    // and a weight that is assumed differ only where |w| and 1 disagree about
    // reaching ZERO, so ±1 in EITHER direction alone is not enough to tell
    // them apart: +2 then −2 empties the entry under both readings.
    //
    //   value 7 @ +2, value 4 @ +1, then value 7 @ −1
    //     weight read:    {7:1, 4:1}  →  lo = 4, hi = 7
    //     ±1 assumed:     {4:1}       →  lo = 4, hi = 4   (7 evicted a step early)
    //
    // then the mirror, so a break in ONE arm cannot hide behind the other —
    // `lo` and `hi` are separate aggregate slots with separate maps:
    //
    //   value 4 @ −1
    //     weight read:    {7:1}       →  lo = 7, hi = 7
    //     ±1 assumed:     {}          →  the group empties and the row vanishes
    let mut hm2: QmIncr = qm_incremental();
    let j7: Vec<S> = one_row(0i64, 7i64);
    let j4: Vec<S> = one_row(0i64, 4i64);
    match qm_apply(&mut hm2, 0i32, 2i64, j7.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 10i32; }
    }
    match qm_apply(&mut hm2, 0i32, 1i64, j4.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 10i32; }
    }
    match qm_apply(&mut hm2, 0i32, 0i64 - 1i64, j7.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 10i32; }
    }
    let j0: Vec<(i64, i64, i64, i64)> = match qm_snapshot(&hm2) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 10i32; }
    };
    t.rows = t.rows + j0.len();
    if bad(t, j0.len() != 1i64) { return 92i32; }
    // count is a SCALAR arm and is right under either reading — asserted so a
    // failure below is unambiguously the MAP's and not the group counter's.
    if bad(t, j0.get(0i64).1 != 2i64) { return 79i32; }
    if bad(t, j0.get(0i64).2 != 4i64) { return 80i32; }
    if bad(t, j0.get(0i64).3 != 7i64) { return 83i32; }   // ← the max arm's discriminator
    match qm_apply(&mut hm2, 0i32, 0i64 - 1i64, j4.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 10i32; }
    }
    let j1: Vec<(i64, i64, i64, i64)> = match qm_snapshot(&hm2) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 10i32; }
    };
    t.rows = t.rows + j1.len();
    if bad(t, j1.len() != 1i64) { return 84i32; }
    if bad(t, j1.get(0i64).1 != 1i64) { return 87i32; }
    if bad(t, j1.get(0i64).2 != 7i64) { return 88i32; }   // ← the min arm's discriminator
    if bad(t, j1.get(0i64).3 != 7i64) { return 91i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
